            <div xml:id="d1023">

                <pb facs="0906" n="899" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg899"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.943/14a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Tientsin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-09T17:00:00-05:00">December 9, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>103. The Department of Agriculture has received a telegram from its Commissioner
                    in Shanghai quoting information received from the Consulate General at Tientsin
                    which reads in part as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Large quantity of Japanese milled flour has been imported into North
                            China through Tientsin. It is estimated during October about 700,000
                            bags were imported and total amount imported since July approximately
                            1,500,000 bags. Importation of other foreign flour is at present
                            impossible as Japanese flour is exempt from regular import custom
                            duty.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Please telegraph whether or not the statement regarding Japanese exemption from
                    customs duty applies to commercial shipments (as distinct from military
                    supplies) and also whether commercial shipments of other Japanese products are
                    being exempted from ordinary customs duties.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
